template:
You are writing invented text for a fictional multilateral negotiation. Everything is fictional: the parties, the agreement and every word of it. Do not reproduce, quote or imitate the wording of any real agreement, treaty, resolution or negotiation you know, and do not name any real country, organisation or person.

The negotiation is about {setting}.

The parties (a coalition speaks for several members as one party):
{parties}

Part 1. Write a short title for each of these articles of the draft:
{titles}

Part 2. Write the text of these articles.
{articles}

Part 3. Write statements that parties made during the talks. Each statement slot names the party, the round of talks in which it spoke, and what it speaks about. For every slot write all four versions below; each version is one or two sentences in the party's own voice, and the four versions must clearly differ in what the party wants:
- in_favour: the party supports the wording it speaks about;
- against: the party objects to that wording;
- firm_limit: the party objects to that wording and says plainly that this is a limit it will not cross (for example, that it cannot accept it in any form);
- accepting: the party accepts that wording as it stands and agrees to it.
{statements}

Rules for every text you write:
- Use plain sentences. Never use square brackets, never use the words option, alternative or alt, and never number alternatives.
- Never write an identifier such as S1, A3 or A3.1 inside a text; refer to wording by what it says.
- Two wordings of the same article must not share any run of five or more words with each other or with that article's lead-in sentence.
- A revision keeps one unbroken stretch of about half of its wording's words exactly as written, and rewrites the rest.
- Say nothing about what happens to any wording later in the talks.
{bands}

Return one JSON object and nothing else, in exactly this form (every slot named above, no other field):
{reply_form}
